-- Five articles published 2026-10-19. Generated by:
--   python3 tools/articles_sql.py --since 2026-10-19
-- An upsert on slug, so re-running it refreshes the text and keeps every id.
insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at)
values
  ('saving-for-something-when-everything-is-urgent', 'Saving for Something When Everything Is Urgent', 'Every spare dollar gets eaten by the car, the dentist or the boiler, and nothing ever accumulates. How to put something aside when there is always something.', 'You decided in January that this year you would put money aside. Then the car needed tires, the dentist found something, the washing machine made a noise, and here you are in October with the same nothing.

It is not that you were careless. It is that every month arrived with a reason, and all of the reasons were real.

Why nothing ever accumulates

The usual advice assumes a flat month: income, outgoings, a little left over. Most people''s months are not flat.

They have an annual shape full of lumps: insurance, car maintenance, birthdays, school things, Christmas, the vet, the appointment you put off. None of those are emergencies. They are entirely predictable, and they arrive without a plan attached.

So they come out of whatever is in the account that week, which is also the money you were going to save. The savings never fail because of a crisis; they fail because of the ordinary year.

Name the lumps and give them their own money

This is the one change that does more than any amount of discipline.

Write down everything that happens once or twice a year rather than monthly: car tax and service, tires, insurance, dental, glasses, holidays, birthdays and Christmas, school uniform, subscriptions billed annually, the thing your boiler needs.

Add them up and divide by twelve. That number is a monthly bill, not an optional saving, and it is the amount that has been quietly eating your savings all year.

Then keep it somewhere separate. A second account, a named pot, an envelope, whatever your bank gives you. Separate matters more than clever, because money in the main account is spending money however you label it in your head.

Start far smaller than feels worthwhile

The temptation is to decide a sensible amount, which is usually one you cannot sustain, which fails in month three and confirms that saving is not for you.

Twenty a month that survives a year beats two hundred a month that lasts six weeks. Five a week counts. Loose change counts.

The purpose of the early months is not the balance; it is proving that money can leave your account and not come back, which is a different skill from intending to save.

Make it automatic and awkward to reverse

Set a standing transfer for the day after you are paid. Money that moves before you look at it does not have to be resisted.

And add one small piece of friction in the other direction: a pot that takes a day to move back, an account without a card, a different bank. Not locked away, because you will need it; just slow enough that a tired Tuesday evening cannot empty it.

If your income is irregular — shifts, self-employment, commission — do it by percentage rather than amount, and move it on the day money arrives rather than on a date.

Keep a small buffer before any long-term saving

Most people try to save for a goal while having nothing for the unexpected, which is why the goal keeps getting raided.

So the first target is a small cushion: a few hundred, enough for a tire, a vet bill, a broken phone. That is what stops every small shock becoming either debt or the end of your savings.

It feels unexciting next to a proper goal, and it is the thing that makes the proper goal possible.

Give the money a name

Saving in the abstract loses to anything concrete. Saving for something specific holds up much better.

So name the pots: Car. Christmas. The trip. Dentist. New sofa. Leaving fund. Whatever it actually is.

And where the goal is distant, put a date and a number on it, then work backward to the monthly figure. Two thousand by next summer is nine months at two hundred and twenty, which is a decision you can make rather than a wish.

If the money genuinely is not there

Sometimes the honest arithmetic says there is nothing to save, and no amount of organizing changes that.

In that case the useful work is elsewhere: checking whether you are getting everything you are entitled to, which is very commonly not the case; a free debt advice service if interest is eating the difference; a conversation about hours or pay; help with a specific cost like childcare, energy or prescriptions.

A benefits check and an hour with a free adviser are worth more than another budgeting app. Neither is a comment on how you handle money.

Expect to raid it, and plan for that

You will take money out. The car will need something, the boiler will go.

That is not a failure; it is the system working. The pot existed so that this would not become a problem, and a pot that empties and refills is doing its job.

What matters is the refilling. Put the transfer back as soon as the month allows, and do not treat the dip as evidence that saving is pointless.

And notice what has changed even when the balance looks the same: a year in which four unexpected things did not become debt is a year that went differently.

When money is more than a numbers problem

Please talk to a doctor or a therapist if money worry is keeping you awake, if you cannot bear to look at your account, or if the dread has spread into most of your week; that is extremely common and it is treatable, and the practical advice works much better once it lifts a little.

And if you are in debt that is growing faster than you can pay it, talk to a free debt advice service rather than carrying it alone. A professional adviser deals with this every day, they can talk to creditors on your behalf, and getting advice early makes a far bigger difference than getting it when things have become urgent.', array['Money', 'Goals']::text[], 0, '2026-10-19T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('grieving-someone-nobody-else-knew', 'Grieving Someone Nobody Else Knew', 'An online friend, an ex, a colleague, someone you had not seen in years. How to mourn a person your own life has no official place for.', 'Someone died and you are not sure who to tell. They were a friend from a group online you have never mentioned. Or an ex you still spoke to. Or a colleague from another job, or an aunt the family fell out with, or someone you loved years ago.

There is no funeral you are invited to, nobody at work who knows to be gentle with you, and no obvious reason you should be this upset. And you are.

Grief does not check your credentials

The depth of a loss has very little to do with how it would look on paper.

Someone you spoke to every day for three years in a message thread can matter more than a relative you saw twice. A person who got you through a bad patch holds a particular place regardless of how long ago it was.

What you are grieving is a relationship, and only you know what was in it.

Why the lack of recognition hurts separately

Most of what makes grief bearable is other people doing things: knowing, asking, bringing food, sitting with you, marking the date.

When nobody around you knew the person, none of that machinery starts. You carry it in a normal week, among people who have no idea why you are quiet, which is a second loss sitting on top of the first.

There is often an extra sting too: the sense that you are not entitled to this. That belongs to the family, you were only a colleague, you had not spoken in years. Disenfranchised grief is the term, and the useful part of knowing it has a name is that it stops you arguing with yourself about whether you are allowed.

Tell at least one person

The silence is the part you can actually change, and it changes more than you would expect.

Pick someone who will not require an explanation of the whole history: a partner, a friend, a sibling. Say it plainly. Somebody died who mattered to me and I do not really have anywhere to put it.

If there is nobody close who fits, the people who shared the person are often the right ones — the group chat, the forum, the old team. Grief shared with people who knew them does something no amount of general sympathy can.

And where the only option is a stranger, a bereavement helpline or a counselor is a reasonable choice rather than an overreaction.

Make your own marker

Without a funeral or an anniversary that anyone else observes, the loss can stay oddly unfinished.

So do something deliberate. Write to them, in full, and keep it or burn it. Go somewhere you associate with them. Cook the thing they liked. Plant something. Give to something they cared about. Put their photograph somewhere for a week.

Pick a date and mark it in your diary — their birthday, the day you heard — so the year has a place for them rather than ambushing you.

Rituals look small from outside. They do a surprising amount of the work.

If you want to reach the family

Sometimes you want contact, and sometimes you are not sure it is your place.

A short note is almost always welcome and almost never intrusive: I worked with him for four years and he was kind to me when I was new, I am so sorry. Families keep those. You are giving them a piece of their person they did not have.

You do not have to attend anything, ask for anything, or explain who you are beyond a line. And if the relationship was one the family did not know about or did not approve of, it is reasonable to stay away and do your grieving privately; that is a protection rather than a denial.

Expect it to be complicated

Many of these losses are tangled, which is part of why they are hard to speak about.

An ex you still loved, or did not. Someone who hurt you. A person you had cut off. Someone you meant to contact and did not, which brings guilt sitting next to the sadness.

All of that can be true at once. Grief about a complicated person is usually a mixture rather than one feeling, and trying to resolve it into something tidy is what keeps people stuck.

Allow it the time it needs

Because nobody knows, there is a temptation to give yourself a weekend and then carry on.

That rarely works. Plan for it to turn up unexpectedly for a while: in the car, at the supermarket, in the hour before sleep. Lower the week''s demands if you can, the way you would if the loss were recognized.

And if you need a day, take a day. You do not have to say whose funeral it is.

When to get help with it

Please talk to a doctor or a therapist if weeks are passing with no change, if you cannot work or sleep, if you are drinking more, or if the feeling of being unentitled to the grief is keeping you from saying it out loud to anyone.

Bereavement services will see you for any loss, including one with no official status: an online friend, an ex, a pet, a colleague, someone you never met in person. Nobody there will ask whether you qualify, and counseling for exactly this is both common and effective.

And if it has taken you somewhere dark, and you are not sure you want to be here, please tell a doctor or someone you trust today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Grief', 'Loneliness']::text[], 0, '2026-10-19T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('the-first-ten-minutes-of-your-day', 'The First Ten Minutes of Your Day', 'You reach for the phone before your eyes are open and arrive at breakfast already behind. A small, practical change to the start of the day, and why it is worth the effort.', 'The alarm goes off, which is also your phone, so the first thing in your hand is a screen. Within ninety seconds you have seen a headline, two work emails and something irritating from somebody you went to school with.

You have not got out of bed yet and the day already has a tone.

Why the first few minutes carry weight

Whatever you look at first sets the frame for everything after it, mostly because waking up leaves you unusually open.

For a short while after waking, your attention has not settled and your mood has not formed. Something sharp — a demanding message, bad news, a notification about money — lands with more force than it would at four in the afternoon, and the alertness it produces tends to last.

And it starts the day in a reactive position: responding to other people''s requests before you have had a single thought of your own.

That is not a reason to be mystical about mornings. It is a reason to be slightly deliberate about ten minutes.

Get the phone out of the first stretch

The whole change, in practice, is this: do not look at the phone until you have been up for a bit.

Buy a cheap alarm clock and charge the phone in another room, which is the version that actually works. Failing that, put it across the room, in a drawer, face down with notifications off overnight.

If you need it reachable for emergencies, keep it on do not disturb with a couple of people allowed through. That covers the genuine case and removes the other hundred things.

And decide what counts as the end of the protected stretch: after you are dressed, after the first drink, after the dog. A line, so you are not negotiating at six in the morning.

Put something in the gap

Removing the phone leaves a space, and an empty space tends to get filled by the phone again. So have something ready.

Water, then whatever hot drink you like, made slowly. Standing outside for two minutes, which does more for waking up than anything on a screen. Opening the curtains and letting daylight hit your eyes. Stretching badly for a minute. Making the bed. Feeding an animal. Writing three lines in a notebook.

None of this needs to be a routine with a name. One or two things, in the same order, is plenty.

The point is that the first thing you do is something you chose.

Decide the one thing before the world asks

If you want a single addition beyond the physical, make it this: before you open anything, decide what matters today.

One line, out loud or on paper: today I want to get the report done, or today I want to be patient with the children, or today I only have to get through it.

That takes twenty seconds and it changes what the emails feel like, because you arrive at them with a priority rather than receiving one.

Expect the first week to be uncomfortable

If you have started your days on a screen for years, the quiet will feel strange, and the urge to check is strongest in exactly the moments you have just freed up.

Notice the urge, let it be there, and go and do the next small thing. It fades over a week or two, and the first thing most people report is not peacefulness but a feeling of having more time, which is simply the time the scrolling was taking.

And expect to fail some mornings. The aim is most days, not every day; one bad morning does not require a fresh start on the first of the month.

What about the news

The news will still be there at nine, and so will the emails.

For most people, moving both to after breakfast costs nothing real and removes a daily spike of alarm about things they cannot act on before work. If your job genuinely requires an early check, do that one thing on purpose, with a time limit, rather than drifting through everything else on the way.

Keep the end of the day in mind too

The morning is downstream of the night before.

Leaving the phone out of the bedroom fixes both ends at once, which is why it is the single change worth the awkwardness. It also tends to improve sleep, because the last ten minutes of a day set a tone in much the same way the first ten do.

And set out whatever the morning needs — clothes, bag, the kettle filled — so the first stretch has as few decisions in it as possible.

When mornings are harder than this

Please talk to a doctor if getting up has become genuinely difficult rather than unpleasant: if you are exhausted however long you sleep, if the first hours are flattened by dread, or if your mood is low and has been for more than a couple of weeks.

Those are common and treatable, and no amount of rearranging a morning routine substitutes for having them looked at. A therapist can also help with the specific pattern of waking up already braced, which often has something behind it worth knowing about.', array['Habits', 'Mindfulness']::text[], 0, '2026-10-19T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-everything-feels-too-loud', 'When Everything Feels Too Loud', 'The strip lights, the radio, somebody chewing, three people needing you at once. What sensory overload is, how to get out of it quickly, and how to have fewer of these afternoons.', 'The extractor fan is going, the radio is on, someone is asking you a question while a child talks over them, and the kitchen light is the kind that hums.

Something in you reaches a limit. You snap, or go silent and rigid, or walk out of the room, and afterward you cannot explain what the problem was, because nothing happened.

What is actually going on

Your nervous system is handling every sound, light, smell and touch in the room, and all of it costs something.

Most of the time that cost is invisible. When the input is high and your capacity is low — tired, hungry, ill, stressed, in pain, already holding a lot — the total goes over what you can process, and the body treats that as a threat.

So you get the alarm response: irritability, a strong urge to escape, a tight chest, sometimes tears, sometimes a kind of shutdown where you cannot speak or decide anything.

It is not rudeness, drama or weakness. It is capacity, and it varies enormously between people and from day to day.

Get out first, understand later

In the moment, explaining is not the job. Reducing input is.

Leave the room. The bathroom, the car, the garden, the stairwell, outside the shop. Thirty seconds of less helps more than a minute of trying to cope with more.

Then cut the biggest channel. Noise is usually it: earplugs, headphones, the fan off, the radio off, the extractor off. Light next: turn the overhead light off, use a lamp, sunglasses in a supermarket, which looks odd and works.

Make yourself physically simpler. Cold water on your wrists or face. Something heavy over your lap or shoulders. Press your feet into the floor. Breathe out longer than you breathe in.

And do not make any decisions for a few minutes. Decisions are the first thing to go and the last thing to come back.

Say the short version out loud

Other people cannot see what is happening, so they read a snapped sentence as being about them.

Have one line ready and use it before it is urgent: too much noise, I need five minutes. Or: I am not cross with you, there is too much going on, I will be back.

Tell the people you live with what it is, once, in a calm hour. Most are relieved to have an explanation, and some will recognize it in themselves.

With children, keep it simple and unfrightening: my ears are full, I am going to stand outside for a minute.

Reduce the baseline rather than coping harder

Most of this is won in advance by lowering the ordinary level of input in your day.

Deal with the permanent noise: a noisy extractor, the radio nobody is listening to, notification sounds, a ticking clock, the television on for company. Turn off what nobody is using.

Change the light where you can. Overhead fluorescent strips are the worst offenders; a lamp, a warmer bulb, or a desk away from flicker makes a real difference.

Keep earplugs and headphones where you will actually have them: by the door, in the car, in a bag. The ones that take the edge off rather than block everything are usually the most useful.

And watch the stacking. Supermarket, then a phone call, then cooking while the children are hungry is three hard things in a row; shifting one of them is easier than enduring all three.

Protect the predictable overload points

Everyone has particular situations. Learn yours and plan for them rather than being surprised.

Supermarkets at busy hours. Open-plan offices. Parties. Soft play. Trains. Family gatherings in one room. Cooking with company. Christmas.

For each one: go at a quieter time, arrive early, sit at the edge, step outside every half hour, bring headphones, agree an exit with someone, and keep the event shorter than feels polite.

Food, sleep and recovery time are the three things that most change your threshold, and they are not luxuries if this happens to you regularly.

Afterward, do not add a verdict

The hour after is often full of shame: I ruined the afternoon, there is something wrong with me, everyone else manages.

Keep it factual instead: it was loud, I was tired, I got overloaded, I went outside and it passed. Then repair anything that needs repairing in one sentence and let it go.

And look at the conditions rather than your character, because the conditions are the part you can change.

When to get help with it

Please talk to a doctor if this is new, getting worse, or coming with headaches, dizziness, ringing in your ears or changes in your hearing or vision; sensitivity to light and sound has physical causes worth checking, including migraine, concussion, ear problems and some medications.

It is also worth a conversation if sensory overload is shaping your life: avoiding places, leaving jobs, dreading family events. Being highly sensitive to input is a known and ordinary way for a nervous system to be — it is a common part of how autistic and attention-different people experience the world, and it also follows burnout, illness and long stretches of stress — and support for it is practical rather than vague.

A therapist can help with the overwhelm and the shame around it, and an occupational therapist is often the most useful professional of all here, because their work is exactly the business of adjusting an environment so a person can function in it.', array['Grounding', 'Emotion Regulation']::text[], 0, '2026-10-19T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('cooking-for-one-again', 'Cooking for One Again', 'The recipes are for four, the portions are wrong, and the point of making dinner has gone. How to feed yourself properly when the household has shrunk.', 'You still buy the big bag of potatoes. The chicken is for a family. You have made a proper meal twice this month, and the other nights were toast standing up, or cereal, or nothing much at all.

Cooking for one after years of cooking for other people is not a smaller version of the same job. It is a different one, and nobody warns you.

Why the point goes out of it

Cooking for other people has a reason built in: they are hungry, they are coming to the table, they will say something about it.

On your own, all of that goes. Nobody notices, nobody thanks you, and there is no reason the meal has to happen at seven or at all. So it slides, and the sliding is not laziness; the structure that held it up has gone.

If the household shrank because somebody died or left, there is more in it than logistics. Mealtimes are where the absence sits most obviously, and a table laid for one says it out loud every evening.

Start with the table rather than the food

Where you eat changes what you eat, more than any recipe does.

Eating standing at the counter or on your lap in front of a screen tends to produce less food, eaten faster, with no sense of having had a meal. Sitting down at a table, with a plate, for ten minutes, changes the whole thing even when the food is simple.

Put something on: the radio, music, a show you like, a podcast. Many people find company in sound more useful than trying to eat in silence.

And if the table is unbearable because of who used to sit at it, eat somewhere else on purpose. A different room, a chair by the window, the garden. That is a reasonable adjustment rather than giving in.

Cook for two or four and plan the rest

The single most useful change: stop trying to cook single portions.

Make the amount the recipe says, eat one, and treat the others as meals rather than leftovers. Fridge for two days, freezer for later, labeled with what it is.

A pot of something on Sunday is three or four weeknights where the cooking is already done. Nobody who lives alone should be starting from raw ingredients every evening.

Freeze in single portions and in containers you can see into. Future you will eat what is visible and ignore what is a mystery.

Build a short list of easy meals

Five or six things you can make without thinking, from what you keep in. That is enough.

Eggs, in any form. Pasta with something. Baked potato with a tin of something. Soup and good bread. A stir-fry from frozen vegetables. Fish from the freezer with rice. Beans on toast with cheese, which is a meal and not a failure.

Keep the components in: eggs, tinned tomatoes and beans, frozen vegetables, frozen fish, rice, pasta, cheese, something for flavor. A kitchen stocked like that can always produce dinner.

And buy smaller where it saves waste. Frozen vegetables, frozen herbs, half a loaf in the freezer, tinned and dried things that do not go off.

Eat with people on purpose

The hardest part of this is not nutrition. It is that eating alone every night is quietly lowering.

So make some meals social, deliberately: one standing arrangement a week, a lunch rather than a dinner if evenings are hard, a cafe where you sit in, a community meal or lunch club, cooking for somebody occasionally, a video call with a plate in front of you.

Invite people round even though the house is different now. The first few times are strange and it gets easier, and feeding people is one of the better reasons to cook.

Lower the standard without abandoning it

There is a middle between a proper meal and nothing, and that middle is where most of your week should live.

A plate with some protein, something fresh and something warm counts. A sandwich with fruit counts. A shop-bought meal with a side salad is perfectly fine, and so are frozen ready meals if they get you fed.

What matters over months is regular eating rather than good cooking. Three reasonable things a day at roughly the same times does more for your mood than one impressive dinner every couple of weeks.

When it is more than a dull chore

Please talk to a doctor if you have been skipping meals for weeks, if you are losing weight without meaning to, if you have no appetite at all, or if cooking and eating have become impossible rather than tedious.

Those are common after a loss or a big change, and they are treatable; a doctor can also check whether anything physical is involved, and a dietitian or a nurse is a reasonable referral to ask for.

A therapist or a bereavement counselor is the right place for the part that is really about the empty chair, which is usually the actual problem rather than the recipes. And if money is the obstacle, ask at your doctor''s office or a local advice service what exists — meal support, lunch clubs and food help are more widely available than most people realize, and widely unclaimed.', array['Food', 'Life Transitions']::text[], 0, '2026-10-19T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
